  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/816b36f644f3e41d8f48bed2dd49e150d751cb13e082b6a1b9b4ad16973d3d57.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}}$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x+3}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & =x+3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2}-3\\cdot y\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-3(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{20}{9}|=\\frac{20}{9}\\,.\n$$",
